= Solution
{parent=i}

The <cumulative hierarchy> is defined by <transfinite recursion> on the <ordinals>:
$$
\boxed{V_0=\varnothing,\qquad V_{\alpha+1}=\mathcal P(V_\alpha),\qquad V_\lambda=\bigcup_{\beta<\lambda}V_\beta\quad(\lambda\text{ a nonzero limit}).}
$$
The recursion theorem of <ZF> supplies each <set> stage. The whole universe is $V=\bigcup_{\alpha\in\mathrm{Ord}}V_\alpha$, a proper-class <union>. Equivalently, $x\in V_\alpha$ exactly when the rank of $x$ is less than $\alpha$, where $\operatorname{rank}(x)=\sup\{\operatorname{rank}(z)+1:z\in x\}$.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= a
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <set> $T$ is transitive if every element of an element of $T$ is itself in $T$, equivalently each element of $T$ is a <subset> of $T$. Proceed by <transfinite induction>. The <empty set> is transitive. If $T$ is transitive and $u\in v\in\mathcal P(T)$, then $v\subseteq T$ gives $u\in T$, and transitivity of $T$ gives $u\subseteq T$. Thus $u\in\mathcal P(T)$, proving the successor stage transitive. A <union> of <transitive sets> is transitive: if $u\in v\in\bigcup_iT_i$, choose $i$ with $v\in T_i$ and infer $u\in T_i$. This proves the limit step and hence **every $V_\alpha$ is transitive**.

= b
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $x\in V_\alpha$, transitivity of $V_\alpha$ gives $x\subseteq V_\alpha$. Therefore $x\in\mathcal P(V_\alpha)=V_{\alpha+1}$, proving
$$
\boxed{V_\alpha\subseteq V_{\alpha+1}.}
$$
Together with the <union> definition at limits, <transfinite induction> now gives $V_\alpha\subseteq V_\beta$ whenever $\alpha\le\beta$. No rank theorem is needed for this monotonicity argument.

= c
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Induct on $\alpha$. For every $\beta<\alpha$, the induction hypothesis says $\beta\in V_{\beta+1}$, and monotonicity gives $V_{\beta+1}\subseteq V_\alpha$. Thus every element of the <ordinal> $\alpha$ lies in $V_\alpha$, so $\alpha\subseteq V_\alpha$. Taking its <power set> gives
$$
\boxed{\alpha\in\mathcal P(V_\alpha)=V_{\alpha+1}.}
$$
For $\alpha=0$ the inclusion is vacuous and $\varnothing\in\mathcal P(\varnothing)$ supplies the base case. This argument uses <ordinal> membership as strict order and the previously proved hierarchy monotonicity.

= iii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Take a nonzero <limit ordinal> $\lambda$ and $x\in V_\lambda$. Choose $\beta<\lambda$ with $x\in V_\beta$. Transitivity gives $x\subseteq V_\beta$. Hence every actual <subset> of $x$ belongs to $\mathcal P(V_\beta)=V_{\beta+1}$, and the actual <set> $\mathcal P(x)$ belongs to $V_{\beta+2}$. Since $\lambda$ is a limit, $\beta+2<\lambda$, so $\mathcal P(x)\in V_\lambda$. Thus the internal <power set> contains every actual <subset> of $x$, and the <axiom of power set>[power-set axiom] holds in $V_\lambda$.

Here the absoluteness fact used is: every <bounded formula in set theory>[bounded formula] is absolute between <transitive sets> or classes with the actual membership relation. In particular $z\subseteq x$, expressed by $\forall t\in z\,(t\in x)$, has the same meaning internally and externally. The argument supplies the actual <power set> as an internal witness. If zero is included in one's convention for <limit ordinals>, its empty stage has no nonempty first-order domain; the assertion is understood for nonzero limits.

If also $\lambda>\omega$, the preceding ordinal-membership result gives $\omega\in V_{\omega+1}\subseteq V_\lambda$. The actual <set> $\omega$ contains the <empty set> and is closed under $z\mapsto z\cup\{z\}$. These bounded membership and successor conditions are absolute in the transitive stage, so $\omega$ is an internal inductive <set>. Therefore **<Infinity> holds as well**. In contrast $V_\omega$ has only hereditarily finite <sets> and cannot use $\omega$ as an element.

= iv
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

To prove <replacement failure at V omega plus omega>, put $M=V_{\omega+\omega}$. The <set> $\omega$ belongs to $M$, and for every $n\in\omega$ the <ordinal> $\omega+n$ also belongs to $M$. The relation saying that $y$ is obtained by taking $n$ successive <ordinal> successors of $\omega$ is definable, with parameter $\omega$, and is single valued in $M$.

For clarity this does not assume <Replacement> inside $M$. For each finite $n$ its finite computation <sequence> exists in $M$: its entries have rank below $\omega+n+1$, and the finite coding overhead still has rank below $\omega+\omega$. The <bounded formula in set theory>[bounded formula] verifying this finite successor chain is absolute between $M$ and the universe. The ordinary finite induction identifying its unique last entry uses the actual finite <ordinals>, which are the same in $M$. This justifies the internal definable <function> $n\mapsto\omega+n$ without invoking the axiom being tested.

If <Replacement> held there, its range $B=\{\omega+n:n\in\omega\}$ would be an element of $M$. But <ordinal> rank equals the <ordinal>, so
$$
\operatorname{rank}(B)=\sup_{n<\omega}(\omega+n+1)=\omega+\omega.
$$
Every element of $V_{\omega+\omega}$ has strictly smaller rank. This contradiction proves **<Replacement> fails in $V_{\omega+\omega}$**. Each individual output is present; the missing object is the <set> collecting all outputs.

