= Solution
{parent=i}

König's theorem for <cardinal numbers> states that, for a set-indexed family with $\kappa_i<\lambda_i$ at every index,
$$
\boxed{\sum_{i\in I}\kappa_i<\prod_{i\in I}\lambda_i.}
$$
Here the sum is the <cardinality> of a <disjoint union> and the product is the <cardinality> of a <Cartesian product>. Work in <ZFC>. Choose disjoint <sets> $A_i$ of sizes $\kappa_i$ and <sets> $B_i$ of sizes $\lambda_i$. For any <function> $f:\bigsqcup_i A_i\to\prod_iB_i$, its $i$th coordinate values on $A_i$ form a <subset> of $B_i$ of <cardinality> at most $\kappa_i$. Choose $b_i\in B_i$ outside that <subset>, using <choice>. Then $b=(b_i)_i$ cannot equal $f(a)$ for any $a$: if $a\in A_j$, its $j$th coordinate was specifically excluded. Thus there is no <surjection> from the <disjoint union> onto the product.

<cardinal number>[Cardinal comparability] under <choice> now implies the displayed strict inequality. Empty index <set> gives $0<1$ directly; empty individual $A_i$ cause no difficulty, since the corresponding coordinate excludes an empty image. This is the cardinal diagonal theorem, rather than the finitely branching tree result also bearing König's name.

= ii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <generalized continuum hypothesis>[generalized continuum hypothesis] is
$$
\boxed{2^\kappa=\kappa^+\quad\text{for every infinite cardinal }\kappa.}
$$
Equivalently, for every <ordinal> $\alpha$, $2^{\aleph_\alpha}=\aleph_{\alpha+1}$. Here $2^\kappa$ is the <cardinality> of the <power set> of a <set> of <cardinality> $\kappa$, and $\kappa^+$ is the least strictly larger cardinal. The hypothesis says that no intermediate cardinal lies between an <infinite set> and its <power set>.

= iii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= a
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For <GCH arithmetic at aleph omega>, let $\kappa=\aleph_\omega$. Use monotonicity of <cardinal exponentiation>, the law $(a^b)^c=a^{bc}$, and $\kappa\cdot\kappa=\kappa$ for infinite cardinals in <ZFC>. Since $2\le\kappa\le2^\kappa$,
$$
2^\kappa\le\kappa^\kappa\le(2^\kappa)^\kappa=2^{\kappa\cdot\kappa}=2^\kappa.
$$
Thus $\kappa^\kappa=2^\kappa$, and the <generalized continuum hypothesis>[generalized continuum hypothesis] yields
$$
\boxed{\aleph_\omega^{\aleph_\omega}=\aleph_{\omega+1}.}
$$
The lower bound embeds binary-valued <functions> into $\kappa$-valued ones; the upper bound codes each value by a <subset> and combines the resulting pairs of indices. In particular the equality before applying the hypothesis is a <ZFC> equality.

= b
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write again $\kappa=\aleph_\omega$. The <countable> <cardinal sum> $\sum_{n<\omega}\aleph_n$ equals $\kappa$: it is at least their supremum, and at most $\aleph_0\cdot\kappa=\kappa$. König's theorem gives
$$
\kappa<\prod_{n<\omega}\aleph_{n+1}.
$$
The latter product injects into $P=\prod_{n<\omega}\aleph_n$ by fixing coordinate zero and putting its $n$th coordinate in coordinate $n+1$. Conversely $P$ injects into the <set> of all <functions> $\omega\to\kappa$. Therefore
$$
\kappa<P\le\kappa^{\aleph_0}\le\kappa^\kappa=2^\kappa=\kappa^+.
$$
No cardinal lies strictly between $\kappa$ and its successor, so
$$
\boxed{\prod_{n<\omega}\aleph_n=\aleph_{\omega+1}.}
$$
The strict lower bound is essential: replacing the product by the supremum of its factors would incorrectly give $\aleph_\omega$.

= c
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The product from the preceding part injects into $\kappa^{\aleph_0}$, where $\kappa=\aleph_\omega$, because each coordinate value is an <ordinal> below $\kappa$. Combining its strict König lower bound with the exponentiation upper bound gives
$$
\kappa<\kappa^{\aleph_0}\le\kappa^\kappa=\kappa^+.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{\aleph_\omega^{\aleph_0}=\aleph_{\omega+1}.}
$$
Only the instance $2^{\aleph_\omega}=\aleph_{\omega+1}$ of the <generalized continuum hypothesis>[generalized continuum hypothesis] was needed in these three computations, though the question assumes the full hypothesis. No assumption that <countable> exponentiation leaves this <singular cardinal> unchanged is valid: König's theorem rules that out.

